Subject: JSON.parse of an 8-bit string parses the string's own bytes, without a UTF-8 copy of the input

What this does
  - `JSParseState.json_latin1`: in a JSON string literal a byte >= 0x80 is that code point (the fast paths of `json_parse_string` keep such bytes in the 8-bit result; the slow path skips the UTF-8 decode)
  - `JS_ParseJSON_internal2` takes the flag; `JS_ParseJSON_internal` is the UTF-8 form as before
  - `js_json_parse`: a flat 8-bit string, no reviver, not taken by the lazy document path, whose byte after the end is the terminator, is parsed from its own bytes; any failure (a syntax error, out of memory, a stack overflow) clears the exception and the input is parsed again from its UTF-8 copy exactly as before, so every error and its message are the general path's; an uncatchable error is rethrown

Why we need it
  Every JSON.parse converted the whole input to UTF-8 first (JS_ToCStringLen2, 10.2% of parse cycles on the app-traffic benchmark). json_app parse only: -9.5% cycles, -9.9% instructions.

Soundness
  Outside string literals JSON is ASCII, where Latin-1 and UTF-8 are the same bytes; inside one, a Latin-1 byte is its code point, which is what decoding its UTF-8 copy gives. Numbers, keys and structure are read from the same ASCII bytes. Differential, old engine vs new: 60,000 inputs (random nested values with Latin-1, control characters, escapes, astral characters, doubles across 40 decades; half mutated by truncation, insertion, replacement and deletion of structural, numeric and non-ASCII bytes; 25 edge cases) give the same results and the same error names and messages (39,691 parsed, 20,309 errors).

